@defgroup micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cli Command-Line Options
@brief Options for controlling the test output at run time.

@details
The test programs accept options on the command line, which
`runner::initialise (argc, argv)` reads. On platforms without a command
line, the same settings are made with `runner_options`, passed to
`runner::initialise()`.

| Option                               | Effect                                |
| ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------- |
| `--help`, `-h`                       | list the options; run no test         |
| `--reporter=tap`, `--reporter=human` | the output format (TAP by default)    |
| `--colour[=always,never,auto]`       | colour the human-readable output      |
| `--verbose`, `--quiet`, `--silent`   | how much the console displays         |
| `--output-file=<path>`               | write the complete report to a file   |
| `--filter=<pattern>`                 | run only the matching names           |
| `--list-tests`, `--list`             | list the test cases; run none         |

@par Options and application arguments
- The µTest++ options come first. They end at the first argument that
  does not start with a dash, or at the first `--` alone.
- That argument, and all the following ones, are left to the application,
  uninterpreted; `runner::application_arguments()` returns them.
- The `--` that ends the options is not passed on. It is needed before an
  application argument that starts with a dash, which would otherwise be
  taken for an unknown µTest++ option. After the first application
  argument, a `--` is passed on like any other argument.
- The value of an option may be attached (`--reporter=human`) or given as
  the next argument (`--reporter human`), except for `--colour`, whose
  value must be attached.

@par Errors
An unknown option (an argument that starts with a dash before the end of
the options, such as `-x`, `-`, or `-verbose`), an unknown reporter or
colour mode, or a missing option value is an error. It is reported on
`stderr`, on the console, and in the output file, if any; no test runs,
and the exit code is 2. For an unknown option, the console also suggests
`--help`.

@section cli-help Help

`--help`, or its short form `-h` (the only short option), writes the usage
and the list of the options on the console, even with `--silent`, runs no
test, and the exit code is 0. Errors in the other options take priority:
they are reported as usual, and the exit code is 2.

@section cli-reporter Reporter

The output follows the
[TAP](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Test_Anything_Protocol) rules by
default, for the tools that read test results. A human-readable format,
the format of the initial releases, is meant for people reading the
console:
- `--reporter=tap` – the TAP reporter (the default);
- `--reporter=human` – the human-readable reporter.

@section cli-colours Colours

The human-readable reporter highlights the results in colour; the TAP
reporter never does, and the output file never receives colours. The
option is modelled on the `--color` option of the GNU tools, whose
spelling `--color` is also accepted:
- `--colour=auto` – use colours only when `stdout` is a terminal (the
  default); a terminal can be detected only on POSIX platforms;
- `--colour=always`, or `--colour` alone – always use colours, for
  example in the CI systems that display the ANSI escape sequences but
  do not provide a terminal;
- `--colour=never` – never use colours.

As in the GNU tools, the value must be attached with `=`, and the
synonyms `yes` and `force` (for `always`), `no` and `none` (for `never`),
and `tty` and `if-tty` (for `auto`) are also accepted.

For compatibility with Catch2 and Boost UT, `--use-colour=<mode>` and
`--use-colour <mode>`, also spelled `--use-color`, are accepted too, with
`yes`, `no`, `auto`, or any other value of `--colour`.

On platforms without a command line, the mode is set in
`runner_options::colour`.

@section cli-verbosity Verbosity

Four levels select how much the console displays:
- default – one line for each test case that passed, and the details of
  each failed check;
- `--verbose` – every check, passed or failed;
- `--quiet` – only the failed checks, with the names of their suites and
  test cases, and the session totals (and, with the TAP reporter, one
  result line for each suite);
- `--silent` – nothing; only the exit code reports the result.

On platforms without a command line, the level is set in
`runner_options::verbosity_level`. The examples below are the output of
`tests/sources/minimal/src/main.cpp`.

@subsection cli-verbosity-default Default mode

@code{.console}
$ minimal-test --reporter=tap one two

# Running: minimal-test --reporter=tap one two
# Built with ...
TAP version 14
# Subtest: minimal-test
    ok 1 - Check various conditions # { passed, 2 checks }

    1..1
ok 1 - minimal-test # { passed, 2 checks in 1 test case, time: X.XXX ms }

1..1
# { total: 2 checks passed, 0 failed, in 1 test case, 1 test suite, time: X.XXX ms }
@endcode

@subsection cli-verbosity-verbose Verbose mode

Every check is displayed, passed or failed:

@code{.console}
$ minimal-test --verbose --reporter=tap one two

# Running: minimal-test --verbose --reporter=tap one two
# Built with ...
TAP version 14
# Subtest: minimal-test

    # Subtest: Check various conditions
        ok 1 - answer is 42
        ok 2 - condition is true
        1..2
    ok 1 - Check various conditions # { passed, 2 checks }

    1..1
ok 1 - minimal-test # { passed, 2 checks in 1 test case, time: X.XXX ms }

1..1
# { total: 2 checks passed, 0 failed, in 1 test case, 1 test suite, time: X.XXX ms }
@endcode

@subsection cli-verbosity-quiet Quiet mode

Only the failed checks and the session totals are displayed; a run
without failures displays only the totals. The TAP reporter also writes
one `ok` or `not ok` line for each suite, so that the output remains a
valid TAP document, which the TAP tools read as failed when a suite
failed:

@code{.console}
$ minimal-test --quiet --reporter=tap one two

TAP version 14
ok 1 - minimal-test # { passed, 2 checks in 1 test case, time: X.XXX ms }
1..1
# { total: 2 checks passed, 0 failed, in 1 test case, 1 test suite, time: X.XXX ms }
@endcode

A failed check is displayed with its location, after the names of its
suite and of its test cases:
- the human-readable reporter writes the names as the headings of the
  default mode, only for the suites and the test cases with failures;
- the TAP reporter writes a `# <suite> > <test case>` line, followed by
  the failure as TAP comments, so that only the suite result lines are
  test points:

@code{.console}
# Top suite > Failed nested subtest > middle subtest > inner subtest
#                 not ok 1 - inner check fails
#                   ---
#                   condition: "1 == 2"
#                   at:
#                     fileName: checks.cpp
#                     lineNumber: 454
#                   ...
@endcode

To keep the complete report while the console shows only the failures,
add an output file, which always receives the complete report:

@code{.console}
$ sample-test --quiet --output-file=sample-test.tap
@endcode

When exceptions are not enabled and a failed requirement aborts the run,
the results of the aborted test case are displayed in quiet mode too,
since the totals are never reached.

@subsection cli-verbosity-silent Silent mode

Nothing is displayed on the console, and only the exit code reports the
result; the output file, if any, is still written.

@section cli-output-file Output File

- `--output-file=<path>` or `--output-file <path>` – also write the
  report to a file.

The file receives the complete report, whatever the verbosity, with both
reporters, and never receives colours, so it can be passed to other
tools. It is truncated first, so it never keeps the results of an
earlier run, and it also receives the option errors. If the file cannot
be opened, the error is reported on `stderr` and on the console, no test
runs, and the exit code is 2.

On platforms without a command line, the path is set in
`runner_options::output_file_path`.

@section cli-filter Filter

- `--filter=<pattern>` or `--filter <pattern>` – run only the suites and
  the test cases whose names match the patterns, as `--grep` does in
  node-tap.

The option is repeated for each nesting level:
- the first pattern applies to the names of the suites;
- the second pattern applies to the names of their test cases;
- each further pattern applies to the names of the test cases nested one
  level deeper.

The levels without a pattern are not filtered; to filter only the test
cases, use `*` for the suites.

In a pattern, `*` matches any sequence of characters, including an empty
one, `?` matches any single character, and `|` separates alternatives.
The match is case-sensitive, and the pattern must match the whole name;
for example, `Static` does not select a suite named `Static suite`, but
`Static*` and `Args*|Static*` do. Quote the patterns, so that the shell
does not expand the wildcards.

The suites and the test cases that are not selected are reported as
skipped, keep their numbers in the TAP plans, and their code does not
run. The top suite is never skipped as a whole, since its body is
`main()`, but its test cases are filtered like those of the other suites.
When the filters select no test case at all, the error is reported, and
the exit code is 2.

On platforms without a command line, the patterns are set in
`runner_options::filter_patterns`.

@par Examples
The `sample` test program has a top suite named `Sample`, and two other
suites, `Args suite` and `Static suite`. To run only the suites whose
names start with `Static`:

@code{.console}
$ sample-test --filter='Static*' one two
...
TAP version 14
# Subtest: Sample
    ok 1 - Check various conditions # SKIP filtered
    ...
    ok 9 - Check containers # SKIP filtered
    1..9
ok 1 - Sample # { passed, 0 checks in 0 test cases, 9 skipped, time: X.XXX ms }

ok 2 - Args suite # SKIP filtered

# Subtest: Static suite
    ok 1 - Check one # { passed, 1 check }
    ok 2 - Check two # { passed, 1 check }

    1..2
ok 3 - Static suite # { passed, 2 checks in 2 test cases, time: X.XXX ms }

1..3
# { total: 2 checks passed, 0 failed, in 2 test cases, 9 skipped, 3 test suites, 1 skipped, time: X.XXX ms }
@endcode

The test cases of the top suite are skipped, since the name `Sample` does
not match the first pattern. To run only the test case `Check one` of the
same suite:

@code{.console}
$ sample-test --filter='Static*' --filter='Check one' one two
...
# Subtest: Static suite
    ok 1 - Check one # { passed, 1 check }
    ok 2 - Check two # SKIP filtered

    1..2
ok 3 - Static suite # { passed, 1 check in 1 test case, 1 skipped, time: X.XXX ms }

1..3
# { total: 1 check passed, 0 failed, in 1 test case, 10 skipped, 3 test suites, 1 skipped, time: X.XXX ms }
@endcode

@section cli-list-tests Listing the Test Cases

- `--list-tests`, or `--list` – list the test cases instead of running
  them, as in Catch2 and Boost UT.

The bodies of the suites run, to create their test cases, but the bodies
of the test cases do not, so no check is evaluated. The selected test
cases are listed under the names of their suites, followed by their
count; the nested test cases are not listed, since only the bodies of
their parents create them.

The list is made of plain lines, the same with both reporters and with
all the verbosity levels, and is written to the console and to the output
file, if any. The exit code is 0, or 2 after an option error.

@code{.console}
$ sample-test --list-tests
All available test cases:
  Sample
    Check various conditions
    ...
  Args suite
    args
  Static suite
    Check one
    Check two
12 test cases in 3 test suites
@endcode

With filter patterns, the header is `Matching test cases:`. On platforms
without a command line, the listing is requested with
`runner_options::list_tests`, and `runner::is_listing()` tells whether
the test cases are being listed.
